1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,260
When a family car drifts off the road

2
00:00:02,260 --> 00:00:04,500
at seventy miles per hour and hits a

3
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:07,820
giant green sign, it should split in half.

4
00:00:08,460 --> 00:00:11,320
That steel post weighs over one thousand

5
00:00:11,320 --> 00:00:13,800
pounds, as heavy as a grand piano.

6
00:00:14,560 --> 00:00:17,520
Yet modern highway breakaway signs make

7
00:00:17,520 --> 00:00:19,960
the car roll right through with hardly a

8
00:00:19,960 --> 00:00:20,220
scratch.

9
00:00:24,180 --> 00:00:26,520
That happens because these posts are built

10
00:00:26,520 --> 00:00:28,880
with a mechanical trapdoor right at the

11
00:00:28,880 --> 00:00:31,840
bottom. The metal joint lets go in under

12
00:00:31,840 --> 00:00:34,320
fifty milliseconds, faster than you can

13
00:00:34,320 --> 00:00:34,600
blink.

14
00:00:35,360 --> 00:00:38,160
Then the whole thousand-pound post swings

15
00:00:38,160 --> 00:00:40,720
straight up, soaring right over your car

16
00:00:40,720 --> 00:00:42,160
roof like a pole vaulter.

17
00:00:43,747 --> 00:00:45,927
Most drivers assume road signs are

18
00:00:45,927 --> 00:00:48,067
anchored deep into the dirt so they never

19
00:00:48,067 --> 00:00:51,207
budge. But building posts that refuse to

20
00:00:51,207 --> 00:00:52,987
move turned out to be one of the

21
00:00:52,987 --> 00:00:55,627
deadliest design mistakes in road history.

22
00:00:56,367 --> 00:00:58,727
So how did engineers discover that making

23
00:00:58,727 --> 00:01:01,547
signs weaker would actually save lives?

24
00:01:03,167 --> 00:01:05,767
Back in the nineteen fifties and sixties,

25
00:01:05,827 --> 00:01:08,167
workers built thousands of miles of new

26
00:01:08,167 --> 00:01:09,487
American interstate highways.

27
00:01:10,207 --> 00:01:12,387
They treated roadside signs like tall

28
00:01:12,387 --> 00:01:13,147
office buildings.

29
00:01:13,767 --> 00:01:16,247
They set thick steel beams into concrete

30
00:01:16,247 --> 00:01:18,647
footings that reached five feet into the

31
00:01:18,647 --> 00:01:20,867
earth, deeper than a kitchen table is

32
00:01:20,867 --> 00:01:21,187
tall.

33
00:01:22,717 --> 00:01:24,737
Those heavy posts easily survived

34
00:01:24,737 --> 00:01:27,737
eighty-mile-per-hour windstorms, but cars

35
00:01:27,737 --> 00:01:29,917
drift off the road every single day.

36
00:01:30,717 --> 00:01:33,177
When a family car struck an unmoving steel

37
00:01:33,177 --> 00:01:35,877
girder at highway speed, the post acted

38
00:01:35,877 --> 00:01:38,077
like a giant stationary ax blade.

39
00:01:38,757 --> 00:01:40,997
It sliced through the car cabin, killing

40
00:01:40,997 --> 00:01:42,977
thousands of drivers every year.

41
00:01:44,337 --> 00:01:45,437
By nineteen sixty-three,

42
00:01:46,137 --> 00:01:47,897
researchers at the Texas Transportation

43
00:01:47,897 --> 00:01:50,497
Institute realized that stopping a car

44
00:01:50,497 --> 00:01:52,097
cold was the wrong goal.

45
00:01:52,837 --> 00:01:55,057
Highway signs should give directions, not

46
00:01:55,057 --> 00:01:56,197
take human lives.

47
00:01:56,857 --> 00:01:59,577
So on an empty Texas airfield, engineers

48
00:01:59,577 --> 00:02:01,957
began deliberately crashing cars into

49
00:02:01,957 --> 00:02:04,457
experimental signposts to make steel

50
00:02:04,457 --> 00:02:05,717
surrender on command.

51
00:02:06,567 --> 00:02:08,987
Their first experiments tried drilling big

52
00:02:08,987 --> 00:02:11,547
holes through wooden posts so they would

53
00:02:11,547 --> 00:02:12,947
snap on bumper impact.

54
00:02:13,687 --> 00:02:16,547
That worked fine for small stop signs, but

55
00:02:16,547 --> 00:02:19,007
it failed completely on giant highway

56
00:02:19,007 --> 00:02:19,507
boards.

57
00:02:20,187 --> 00:02:23,027
Those huge green signs catch wind like the

58
00:02:23,027 --> 00:02:25,787
sail of a ship, so weakened posts simply

59
00:02:25,787 --> 00:02:27,547
blew over in thunderstorms.

60
00:02:28,640 --> 00:02:31,040
Next, the researchers tried springs,

61
00:02:31,580 --> 00:02:34,760
cables, and even tiny explosive bolts that

62
00:02:34,760 --> 00:02:36,220
would fire during a crash.

63
00:02:36,520 --> 00:02:39,920
But road salt, rain, and winter frost

64
00:02:39,920 --> 00:02:42,280
rusted those delicate parts within two

65
00:02:42,280 --> 00:02:44,660
seasons. Road crews simply could not

66
00:02:44,660 --> 00:02:47,440
afford to clean and grease complex gadgets

67
00:02:47,440 --> 00:02:49,260
on thousands of remote posts.

68
00:02:50,730 --> 00:02:52,790
The real breakthrough came when

69
00:02:52,790 --> 00:02:54,790
researchers stopped trying to snap the

70
00:02:54,790 --> 00:02:55,690
steel entirely.

71
00:02:56,350 --> 00:02:58,230
What if they cut the post in half

72
00:02:58,230 --> 00:03:00,490
beforehand, and clamped the two pieces

73
00:03:00,490 --> 00:03:03,630
together? That single joint could shrug

74
00:03:03,630 --> 00:03:06,490
off hurricane winds, yet slide apart the

75
00:03:06,490 --> 00:03:08,090
second a car bumper nudges it.

76
00:03:09,622 --> 00:03:10,652
In nineteen sixty-five,

77
00:03:11,332 --> 00:03:13,372
Texas crews installed these experimental

78
00:03:13,372 --> 00:03:15,592
slide-apart joints on real public

79
00:03:15,592 --> 00:03:16,012
highways.

80
00:03:16,692 --> 00:03:19,232
Just months later, a car veered off the

81
00:03:19,232 --> 00:03:21,252
road and hit one at over sixty miles

82
00:03:21,252 --> 00:03:24,112
per hour. The sign popped clean off its

83
00:03:24,112 --> 00:03:26,472
base, sailed over the roof, and the driver

84
00:03:26,472 --> 00:03:27,792
walked away unhurt.

85
00:03:28,400 --> 00:03:31,500
Because of that miraculous crash, federal

86
00:03:31,500 --> 00:03:33,200
highway officials soon made this

87
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:35,780
slide-apart design mandatory on every

88
00:03:35,780 --> 00:03:37,020
major American highway.

89
00:03:37,920 --> 00:03:40,040
It looks like a single heavy girder

90
00:03:40,040 --> 00:03:42,140
standing in the grass, but it hides a

91
00:03:42,140 --> 00:03:43,040
brilliant trick.

92
00:03:43,760 --> 00:03:45,900
So how does this simple metal base know

93
00:03:45,900 --> 00:03:47,860
the difference between wind and a car?

94
00:03:49,247 --> 00:03:51,387
To see the trick, look closely at the

95
00:03:51,387 --> 00:03:53,467
bottom of the post, about four inches

96
00:03:53,467 --> 00:03:55,487
above the concrete pad, roughly the height

97
00:03:55,487 --> 00:03:56,187
of a soda can.

98
00:03:56,187 --> 00:03:58,807
The post does not run into the ground

99
00:03:58,807 --> 00:04:02,567
at all. Instead, two flat triangular steel

100
00:04:02,567 --> 00:04:04,847
plates sit pressed together, squeezed

101
00:04:04,847 --> 00:04:06,627
tight by three heavy bolts.

102
00:04:07,434 --> 00:04:08,994
Here is the clever part:

103
00:04:09,694 --> 00:04:12,434
those bolts do not sit in round drilled

104
00:04:12,434 --> 00:04:16,134
holes. They rest inside open slots that

105
00:04:16,134 --> 00:04:18,214
cut straight out to the edge of the

106
00:04:18,214 --> 00:04:18,794
metal plate.

107
00:04:19,534 --> 00:04:22,134
Picture two dinner forks with their prongs

108
00:04:22,134 --> 00:04:22,974
pushed together.

109
00:04:23,674 --> 00:04:25,634
If you pull them straight apart, they

110
00:04:25,634 --> 00:04:28,694
hold, but if you slide them sideways, they

111
00:04:28,694 --> 00:04:30,074
slip free instantly.

112
00:04:31,637 --> 00:04:33,897
Road crews tighten these bolts with a

113
00:04:33,897 --> 00:04:36,197
torque wrench, a special tool that sets

114
00:04:36,197 --> 00:04:37,897
exact squeezing pressure.

115
00:04:38,537 --> 00:04:40,577
The bolts clamp the two metal plates

116
00:04:40,577 --> 00:04:42,777
together with thousands of pounds of

117
00:04:42,777 --> 00:04:45,817
clamping force. That sandpaper-like grip

118
00:04:45,817 --> 00:04:48,157
is easily strong enough to resist a

119
00:04:48,157 --> 00:04:50,457
ninety-mile-per-hour hurricane gust

120
00:04:50,457 --> 00:04:51,417
pushing on the sign.

121
00:04:52,824 --> 00:04:55,084
When storm winds push high up on the

122
00:04:55,084 --> 00:04:57,364
green board, they try to tip the whole

123
00:04:57,364 --> 00:04:58,624
post over backwards.

124
00:04:59,244 --> 00:05:01,564
That tipping motion pulls straight up on

125
00:05:01,564 --> 00:05:03,364
the front steel bolts in tension,

126
00:05:03,704 --> 00:05:05,284
stretching them like rubber bands.

127
00:05:06,084 --> 00:05:08,560
Three steel bolts can resist over thirty

128
00:05:08,560 --> 00:05:11,084
thousand pounds of stretch, as heavy as

129
00:05:11,084 --> 00:05:13,904
five pickup trucks, so the sign stays rock

130
00:05:13,904 --> 00:05:14,324
solid.

131
00:05:15,850 --> 00:05:18,190
Now picture what happens when a car bumper

132
00:05:18,190 --> 00:05:20,410
strikes the post eighteen inches off the

133
00:05:20,410 --> 00:05:22,010
ground, about knee-high.

134
00:05:22,630 --> 00:05:25,530
That impact is not a gentle breeze spread

135
00:05:25,530 --> 00:05:27,730
out across a giant board fifty feet in

136
00:05:27,730 --> 00:05:30,310
the air. It is a violent, concentrated

137
00:05:30,310 --> 00:05:33,210
punch pushed straight sideways against the

138
00:05:33,210 --> 00:05:33,790
bottom plate.

139
00:05:35,117 --> 00:05:37,417
The moment that sideways bumper punch

140
00:05:37,417 --> 00:05:39,657
overpowers the clamping grip between the

141
00:05:39,657 --> 00:05:42,057
plates, the top plate slides forward

142
00:05:42,057 --> 00:05:43,237
across the bottom plate.

143
00:05:43,797 --> 00:05:46,177
Because the bolt holes are open slots, the

144
00:05:46,177 --> 00:05:47,357
bolts never have to break.

145
00:05:47,957 --> 00:05:50,457
The sliding top plate simply knocks all

146
00:05:50,457 --> 00:05:52,437
three bolts right out into the open air.

147
00:05:53,697 --> 00:05:55,957
That whole disconnection takes just thirty

148
00:05:55,957 --> 00:05:58,117
milliseconds, which is about three times

149
00:05:58,117 --> 00:06:00,157
faster than a single human eye blink.

150
00:06:00,757 --> 00:06:02,837
Before your car bumper has even dented

151
00:06:02,837 --> 00:06:05,257
inward two inches, the bottom of the sign

152
00:06:05,257 --> 00:06:07,297
post has completely detached from the

153
00:06:07,297 --> 00:06:07,797
planet Earth.

154
00:06:08,600 --> 00:06:11,600
That quick release solves the base, but it

155
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:14,160
creates a brand new nightmare for anyone

156
00:06:14,160 --> 00:06:15,660
sitting inside the car.

157
00:06:16,280 --> 00:06:18,360
If you simply kick the legs out from

158
00:06:18,360 --> 00:06:21,340
under a thousand-pound steel post, gravity

159
00:06:21,340 --> 00:06:21,940
takes over.

160
00:06:22,700 --> 00:06:25,520
The heavy sign above pauses in mid-air

161
00:06:25,520 --> 00:06:28,600
while the bottom flies forward, aiming a

162
00:06:28,600 --> 00:06:31,120
jagged steel spear straight through your

163
00:06:31,120 --> 00:06:31,480
windshield.

164
00:06:33,173 --> 00:06:35,353
To keep that steel spear from slicing

165
00:06:35,353 --> 00:06:37,753
through the glass, engineers added a

166
00:06:37,753 --> 00:06:40,613
second mechanical trapdoor seven feet up

167
00:06:40,613 --> 00:06:42,393
the beam, just above head height.

168
00:06:42,733 --> 00:06:44,913
It is called an upper fuse plate.

169
00:06:45,773 --> 00:06:48,513
Workers slice a clean horizontal cut

170
00:06:48,513 --> 00:06:50,693
across the front metal face of the steel

171
00:06:50,693 --> 00:06:53,313
post, leaving only the thin back strip

172
00:06:53,313 --> 00:06:53,713
intact.

173
00:06:55,123 --> 00:06:58,263
On calm days, a small steel plate bolted

174
00:06:58,263 --> 00:07:00,383
over the cut keeps the beam stiff against

175
00:07:00,383 --> 00:07:03,223
the wind. But the split second the bottom

176
00:07:03,223 --> 00:07:05,843
slip base kicks loose, the forces on that

177
00:07:05,843 --> 00:07:07,163
cut reverse completely.

178
00:07:08,183 --> 00:07:10,083
The car bumper shoves the lower beam

179
00:07:10,083 --> 00:07:12,583
forward, putting enormous bending stress

180
00:07:12,583 --> 00:07:14,463
directly on that pre-cut notch.

181
00:07:15,983 --> 00:07:18,463
The bolts on that upper patch snap clean

182
00:07:18,463 --> 00:07:20,503
off, and the remaining strip of steel

183
00:07:20,503 --> 00:07:22,483
bends backward like an elbow joint.

184
00:07:23,323 --> 00:07:25,863
Instead of falling down onto the hood, the

185
00:07:25,863 --> 00:07:28,183
bottom half of the post swings upward into

186
00:07:28,183 --> 00:07:28,603
the sky.

187
00:07:29,343 --> 00:07:31,503
It turns the post into an upside-down

188
00:07:31,503 --> 00:07:33,903
pendulum, pivoting harmlessly around that

189
00:07:33,903 --> 00:07:34,543
upper hinge.

190
00:07:35,756 --> 00:07:37,996
Because the post swings up toward the

191
00:07:37,996 --> 00:07:40,496
clouds, it clears the hood and the glass

192
00:07:40,496 --> 00:07:40,996
completely.

193
00:07:42,096 --> 00:07:44,156
By the time your car travels forty feet

194
00:07:44,156 --> 00:07:46,376
past the impact point, about two car

195
00:07:46,376 --> 00:07:48,636
lengths, the heavy beam is hanging high

196
00:07:48,636 --> 00:07:49,056
overhead.

197
00:07:49,976 --> 00:07:52,116
Your car drives safely underneath before

198
00:07:52,116 --> 00:07:53,996
gravity can pull the metal back down.

199
00:07:54,734 --> 00:07:57,474
The timing of this entire chain reaction

200
00:07:57,474 --> 00:07:58,354
is stunning.

201
00:07:59,154 --> 00:08:01,654
The base releases at thirty milliseconds,

202
00:08:02,414 --> 00:08:04,774
the elbow hinge folds at fifty

203
00:08:04,774 --> 00:08:07,874
milliseconds, and the car sails under the

204
00:08:07,874 --> 00:08:10,334
suspended steel by ninety milliseconds.

205
00:08:11,194 --> 00:08:13,694
Inside the car, passengers feel little

206
00:08:13,694 --> 00:08:16,094
more than a dull thump against the bumper.

207
00:08:17,964 --> 00:08:20,644
Many drivers assume these breakaway posts

208
00:08:20,644 --> 00:08:23,264
protect you in every conceivable crash.

209
00:08:23,564 --> 00:08:26,024
They believe the sign will magically

210
00:08:26,024 --> 00:08:28,184
tumble out of the way no matter how

211
00:08:28,184 --> 00:08:28,784
you hit it.

212
00:08:29,384 --> 00:08:31,364
But these systems are strictly

213
00:08:31,364 --> 00:08:34,004
directional, meaning their safety depends

214
00:08:34,004 --> 00:08:36,484
completely on the angle of your impact.

215
00:08:38,037 --> 00:08:40,577
Remember, the open slots in the base

216
00:08:40,577 --> 00:08:42,977
plates face toward oncoming highway

217
00:08:42,977 --> 00:08:45,337
traffic so the bolts can slip free.

218
00:08:46,037 --> 00:08:47,957
If a car spins out on ice and

219
00:08:47,957 --> 00:08:50,317
hits the post from behind, or slides

220
00:08:50,317 --> 00:08:54,637
sideways from a ditch, the bolts cannot

221
00:08:54,637 --> 00:08:56,757
slide out. Instead, they slam hard into

222
00:08:56,757 --> 00:08:58,797
the closed back wall of the slots.

223
00:09:00,254 --> 00:09:02,594
When the bolts get jammed, the slip base

224
00:09:02,594 --> 00:09:03,954
cannot slide at all.

225
00:09:04,614 --> 00:09:06,754
The post suddenly behaves just like the

226
00:09:06,754 --> 00:09:09,484
deadly, rigid steel beams of the nineteen

227
00:09:09,484 --> 00:09:12,414
sixties, folding slowly and crushing the

228
00:09:12,414 --> 00:09:12,954
front cabin.

229
00:09:13,914 --> 00:09:16,814
That is why highway engineers spend weeks

230
00:09:16,814 --> 00:09:19,414
surveying road curves to aim those slots

231
00:09:19,414 --> 00:09:21,154
directly at oncoming traffic.

232
00:09:21,767 --> 00:09:25,227
There is another terrifying trap built

233
00:09:25,227 --> 00:09:25,927
into this physics:

234
00:09:26,687 --> 00:09:28,967
hitting a sign at seventy miles per hour

235
00:09:28,967 --> 00:09:31,727
is often safer than hitting it at twenty.

236
00:09:32,467 --> 00:09:34,867
At highway speeds, your car moves fast

237
00:09:34,867 --> 00:09:36,947
enough to shoot completely under the

238
00:09:36,947 --> 00:09:38,607
swinging post before it drops.

239
00:09:39,207 --> 00:09:41,587
The car acts as its own high-speed getaway

240
00:09:41,587 --> 00:09:41,987
vehicle.

241
00:09:43,600 --> 00:09:46,000
In a low-speed crash, your bumper still

242
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:47,920
has enough punch to knock the slip base

243
00:09:47,920 --> 00:09:50,520
loose. But because you are only rolling at

244
00:09:50,520 --> 00:09:53,060
fifteen miles per hour, your car does not

245
00:09:53,060 --> 00:09:54,160
clear the zone in time.

246
00:09:55,080 --> 00:09:57,840
The thousand-pound beam swings up, stops

247
00:09:57,840 --> 00:09:59,920
at the top of its arc, and crashes

248
00:09:59,920 --> 00:10:01,560
straight down onto your windshield.

249
00:10:02,467 --> 00:10:03,987
Beyond saving lives,

250
00:10:04,647 --> 00:10:07,107
these sacrificial joints also save

251
00:10:07,107 --> 00:10:10,187
taxpayers millions of dollars every single

252
00:10:10,187 --> 00:10:10,587
year.

253
00:10:11,367 --> 00:10:14,267
When an old rigid sign was struck, the

254
00:10:14,267 --> 00:10:16,767
impact shattered the concrete foundation

255
00:10:16,767 --> 00:10:17,727
into rubble.

256
00:10:18,447 --> 00:10:20,787
Fixing it required jackhammers, fresh

257
00:10:20,787 --> 00:10:23,407
concrete pours, and lane closures that

258
00:10:23,407 --> 00:10:25,167
cost over twenty thousand dollars.

259
00:10:26,757 --> 00:10:29,957
With modern breakaway posts, that concrete

260
00:10:29,957 --> 00:10:32,417
footing in the ground is rarely damaged at

261
00:10:32,417 --> 00:10:32,657
all.

262
00:10:33,417 --> 00:10:35,657
A two-person maintenance crew can pull up

263
00:10:35,657 --> 00:10:37,737
in a pickup truck, lift the post back

264
00:10:37,737 --> 00:10:40,097
into place with a small winch, drop in

265
00:10:40,097 --> 00:10:42,577
three new bolts, tighten them down, and

266
00:10:42,577 --> 00:10:44,997
drive away in thirty minutes for under

267
00:10:44,997 --> 00:10:45,777
five hundred dollars.

268
00:10:47,610 --> 00:10:50,250
This shift in thinking created what

269
00:10:50,250 --> 00:10:52,590
planners call the forgiving highway.

270
00:10:53,350 --> 00:10:55,850
Instead of building unyielding armor to

271
00:10:55,850 --> 00:10:58,810
fight nature, engineers accept that humans

272
00:10:58,810 --> 00:11:01,690
make mistakes, blow tires, and drift off

273
00:11:01,690 --> 00:11:02,190
the asphalt.

274
00:11:03,030 --> 00:11:05,730
Every roadside object within thirty feet

275
00:11:05,730 --> 00:11:08,210
of the white line must either yield or

276
00:11:08,210 --> 00:11:09,350
hide behind a barrier.

277
00:11:10,830 --> 00:11:12,770
You can spot this planned failure

278
00:11:12,770 --> 00:11:14,330
everywhere on the road today.

